  "worked_solution": "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=\u0394\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\u0394\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=3$ to $x=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}({x}^{3}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-4x){|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{625}{4}-20)-(\\frac{81}{4}-12) \\\\  & =128\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=10\\,m/s$ at $x=5.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=3$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 128 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 128 & =200-2{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2{v}_{0}^{2} & =72 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=3$ is $6\\,m/s.$",
